    "prompt": "Function `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` in src/click/_winconsole.py. The statements below were written about an earlier version of the function.\n\nCurrent version of the function (docstring removed):\n```python\ndef readinto(self, b: Buffer) -> int:\n        bytes_to_be_read = len(b)\n        if not bytes_to_be_read:\n            return 0\n        elif bytes_to_be_read % 2:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"cannot read odd number of bytes from UTF-16-LE encoded console\"\n            )\n\n        buffer = get_buffer(b, writable=True)\n        code_units_to_be_read = bytes_to_be_read // 2\n        code_units_read = c_ulong()\n\n        rv = ReadConsoleW(\n            HANDLE(self.handle),\n            buffer,\n            code_units_to_be_read,\n            byref(code_units_read),\n            None,\n        )\n        if GetLastError() == ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED:\n            # wait for KeyboardInterrupt\n            time.sleep(0.1)\n        if not rv:\n            raise OSError(_(\"Windows error: {error}\").format(error=GetLastError()))\n\n        if buffer[0] == EOF:\n            return 0\n        return 2 * code_units_read.value\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: readinto(self, b) -> int\ncalls: GetLastError, HANDLE, OSError, ReadConsoleW, ValueError, _, _(\"Windows error: {error}\").format, byref, c_ulong, get_buffer, len, time.sleep\nreturn statements: return 0 | return 0 | return 2 * code_units_read.value\nraise statements: raise ValueError(\n                \"cannot read odd number of bytes from UTF-16-L | raise OSError(_(\"Windows error: {error}\").format(error=GetLastError()))\ncontrol flow: if=4 loops=0 try=0 with=0 returns=3 yields=0 awaits=0\n\nDiff from the previous version to this version:\n```diff\n--- previous\n+++ current\n@@ -23,5 +23,5 @@\n             time.sleep(0.1)\n         if not rv:\n-            raise OSError(f\"Windows error: {GetLastError()}\")\n+            raise OSError(_(\"Windows error: {error}\").format(error=GetLastError()))\n \n         if buffer[0] == EOF:\n```\n\nStatements:\nS1. If the number of bytes to be read is zero, _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto immediately returns 0 without performing any operations.\nS2. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto raises a `ValueError` if the number of bytes is odd.\nS3. If the first character in the buffer is an end-of-file marker (`EOF`), _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto returns 0, indicating no data was read.\nS4. If the read operation is aborted, _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto waits briefly for a keyboard interrupt before proceeding.\nS5. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto returns twice the number of code units read, converting this count back into bytes.\nS6. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto handles errors by raising exceptions (`ValueError` and `OSError`) when encountering invalid input or failed operations, respectively.\nS7. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto takes one parameter, `b`, which is a buffer object.\nS8. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto raises an `OSError` if the read operation fails (indicated by a return value of 0 from `ReadConsoleW`).\nS9. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto calls the `ReadConsoleW` Windows API function with the buffer and other parameters to perform the actual reading operation.\nS10. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto relies on several helper functions and methods: `get_buffer`, `ReadConsoleW`, and `GetLastError`.\nS11. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto uses the `get_buffer` helper function to obtain a writable buffer view of the input object `b`.\nS12. _WindowsConsoleReader.readinto calculates how many code units need to be read by dividing the byte count by 2.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condi